⏳ Topic: Time & Work + Pipes & Cisterns

This is one of the most important aptitude topics for placements.

The secret is simple:

> Don't think in terms of days. Think in terms of WORK DONE PER DAY.




---

1️⃣ The Basic Concept

Suppose A can complete a job in 10 days.

That means:

\[
\boxed{\text{A's 1-day work}=\frac{1}{10}}
\]

Suppose B can complete the same job in 20 days.

\[
\boxed{\text{B's 1-day work}=\frac{1}{20}}
\]


---

🧠 Golden Formula

If a person completes work in \(n\) days:

\[
\boxed{\text{1-day work}=\frac1n}
\]

If a person does \(\frac1n\) work per day:

\[
\boxed{\text{Total days}=n}
\]


---

2️⃣ Working Together ⭐

A finishes a job in 10 days.

B finishes it in 20 days.

Their one-day work:

\[
\frac{1}{10}+\frac{1}{20}
\]

LCM = 20:

\[
\frac{2}{20}+\frac{1}{20}
\]

\[
=\frac3{20}
\]

Together they complete:

\[
\frac3{20}
\]

of the work per day.

Time required:

\[
\frac{1}{3/20}
\]

\[
\boxed{\frac{20}{3}=6.67\text{ days}}
\]


---

🔥 3️⃣ Direct Shortcut

If A takes \(a\) days and B takes \(b\) days:

\[
\boxed{
\text{Together time}=
\frac{ab}{a+b}
}
\]

Example:

A = 10 days
B = 20 days

\[
\frac{10\times20}{10+20}
\]

\[
=\frac{200}{30}
\]

\[
=\boxed{6.67\text{ days}}
\]

⚠️ This shortcut works for two workers doing positive work together.


---

4️⃣ The LCM Method ⭐⭐⭐

This is often the fastest method.

Suppose:

A completes work in 10 days.

B completes work in 15 days.

Take total work as:

\[
LCM(10,15)=30\ units
\]

Therefore:

A's efficiency = 30/10 = 3 units/day
B's efficiency = 30/15 = 2 units/day

Together:

\[
3+2=5
\]

Time:

\[
30/5
\]

\[
\boxed{6\text{ days}}
\]

🔥 For aptitude questions, the LCM method is extremely powerful.


---

5️⃣ Efficiency and Time

Efficiency and time are inversely proportional.

\[
\boxed{
Efficiency\propto\frac1{Time}
}
\]

Suppose:

A takes 10 days.

B takes 20 days.

Then:

\[
A:B\text{ efficiency}
\]

\[
=\frac1{10}:\frac1{20}
\]

\[
=\boxed{2:1}
\]

So A is twice as efficient as B.


---

6️⃣ Efficiency Ratio Trick

If:

\[
A:B\text{ time}=2:3
\]

Then efficiency ratio is:

\[
\boxed{3:2}
\]

Memory:

> Time ratio → Flip it → Efficiency ratio




---

7️⃣ Men × Days = Constant

For the same work:

\[
\boxed{
Men\times Days=Constant
}
\]

Example:

10 workers complete a job in 12 days.

How many days will 15 workers take?

\[
10\times12=15\times x
\]

\[
x=8
\]

\[
\boxed{8\text{ days}}
\]

This works when all workers have the same efficiency.


---

8️⃣ Men–Days–Hours

Sometimes working hours also change.

\[
\boxed{
Men\times Days\times Hours\times Efficiency
}
\]

is proportional to total work.

Example:

10 workers work 8 hours/day for 6 days.

Another group has 12 workers and works 10 hours/day.

How many days for the same work?

\[
10\times8\times6
=
12\times10\times x
\]

\[
480=120x
\]

\[
\boxed{x=4\text{ days}}
\]


---

🔥 9️⃣ Work Done in Some Days

A can finish a job in 12 days.

One-day work:

\[
\frac1{12}
\]

In 4 days:

\[
4\times\frac1{12}
\]

\[
=\boxed{\frac13}
\]

Therefore remaining work:

\[
1-\frac13
\]

\[
=\boxed{\frac23}
\]


---

🔟 A Works First, Then B Joins

A completes work in 10 days.

B completes it in 15 days.

A works alone for 2 days.

Using LCM:

\[
LCM(10,15)=30
\]

Total work = 30 units.

A's daily work:

\[
30/10=3
\]

B's daily work:

\[
30/15=2
\]

A works 2 days:

\[
2\times3=6
\]

Remaining:

\[
30-6=24
\]

Together per day:

\[
3+2=5
\]

Time:

\[
24/5=4.8
\]

Total time:

\[
2+4.8
\]

\[
\boxed{6.8\text{ days}}
\]


---

1️⃣1️⃣ One Worker Leaves

A can do work in 10 days.

B can do work in 15 days.

They work together for 2 days, then B leaves.

LCM method:

Total work:

\[
30
\]

A:

\[
3/day
\]

B:

\[
2/day
\]

Together:

\[
5/day
\]

Work in 2 days:

\[
10
\]

Remaining:

\[
20
\]

A alone:

\[
20/3
\]

\[
=6.67\text{ days}
\]

Total:

\[
2+6.67
\]

\[
\boxed{8.67\text{ days}}
\]


