🚰 1️⃣3️⃣ Pipes & Cisterns

Pipes follow the same Time & Work logic.

A pipe fills a tank in 10 hours.

Its 1-hour work:

\[
\boxed{\frac1{10}}
\]

A pipe filling in 20 hours:

\[
\boxed{\frac1{20}}
\]

Together:

\[
\frac1{10}+\frac1{20}
\]

\[
=\frac3{20}
\]

Tank fills in:

\[
\boxed{6.67\text{ hours}}
\]


---

🚨 1️⃣4️⃣ Outlet / Leak Pipe

An outlet removes work.

So we subtract its rate.

Suppose:

Inlet fills in 10 hours → \(+1/10\)

Outlet empties in 20 hours → \(-1/20\)


Net work:

\[
\frac1{10}-\frac1{20}
\]

\[
=\frac1{20}
\]

Therefore tank fills in:

\[
\boxed{20\text{ hours}}
\]


---

🔥 1️⃣5️⃣ Multiple Pipes

Suppose:

Pipe A fills in 12 hours.

Pipe B fills in 18 hours.

Pipe C empties in 36 hours.


Net rate:

\[
\frac1{12}+\frac1{18}-\frac1{36}
\]

LCM = 36:

\[
\frac3{36}+\frac2{36}-\frac1{36}
\]

\[
=\frac4{36}
\]

\[
=\frac19
\]

Therefore:

\[
\boxed{9\text{ hours}}
\]


---

1️⃣6️⃣ Efficiency Percentage

Suppose A is 50% more efficient than B.

Take B's efficiency = 100 units.

A:

\[
150
\]

Efficiency ratio:

\[
A:B=150:100
\]

\[
=\boxed{3:2}
\]

Since time is inverse:

\[
A:B\text{ time}
\]

\[
=\boxed{2:3}
\]


---

1️⃣7️⃣ If A is Twice as Efficient

Suppose:

A's efficiency = 2 × B.

Then:

\[
Efficiency\ ratio=2:1
\]

Time ratio:

\[
\boxed{1:2}
\]

If B takes 20 days:

A takes:

\[
\boxed{10\text{ days}}
\]


---

🔥 1️⃣8️⃣ The Most Important Mental Model

Think of work as a tank of 100 units.

Example:

A finishes in 10 days

Then:

\[
A=10\ units/day
\]

B finishes in 20 days

Then:

\[
B=5\ units/day
\]

Together:

\[
15\ units/day
\]

Time:

\[
100/15=6.67
\]

This is exactly the same as fractions—but easier to visualize.


